 "text": "There isn’t one universal “best” UK current account for everyday spending — it depends on whether you prioritise cashback, budgeting tools, or simplicity — but the strongest options right now are free app-based accounts from Chase, Monzo, and Starling.\n\n### Best for rewards on everyday spending: Chase\nChase (part of JPMorgan) stands out if you want money back on normal spending. It pays **2% cashback** on eligible UK debit (and credit) card spending in categories such as groceries, restaurants, cafes, takeaways, everyday transport, fuel, and public EV charging, capped at **£20 a month** (so up to £240 a year). \n\nTo qualify each month you generally need at least 15 payments (card or Direct Debit) and £1,000+ held across Chase saver accounts every day of the previous month (new customers get a short grace period). The account itself is free, with fee-free card spending and ATM withdrawals abroad (limits apply), plus competitive linked savers (including a boosted rate for new customers and a 6.5% regular saver). It has no overdraft. MoneySavingExpert highlights it for cashback and savings, and Which? names it a Recommended Provider.\n\n### Best for budgeting and tracking spending: Monzo\nMonzo is widely regarded as the strongest for hands-on control of day-to-day money. The free account includes real-time spending notifications, automatic categories, Pots (to ring-fence money), salary sorting, round-ups, and bill tools. It also offers an overdraft (rates vary by credit score) and fee-free card spending abroad (ATM limits apply on the free plan; higher limits on paid plans). \n\nIt consistently ranks at or near the top in customer service surveys and is a Which? Recommended Provider. MoneySavingExpert calls it the top pick for hands-on budgeting.\n\n### Best simple all-rounder: Starling\nStarling is frequently the top choice for a clean, reliable everyday account. It has no monthly fee and no paid tiers, excellent app features (including Spaces for separating money), relatively competitive overdraft rates, and truly fee-free card spending plus unlimited fee-free ATM withdrawals abroad. Customer scores are consistently among the highest — Which? has recommended it for years (recently scoring around 86%), and it ranks near the top of independent service-quality surveys. MoneySavingExpert lists it as a top all-rounder.\n\n### Other solid options\n- **Nationwide** or **First Direct** if you want branch access or phone support. Both are Which? Recommended Providers. Nationwide has a strong branch network and sometimes useful rewards or interest features; First Direct is long praised for customer service and offers an interest-free overdraft buffer.\n- **Zopa Biscuit** can suit people who want cashback focused on bills or eating out (or interest on the balance), but it’s more of a complementary account for many.\n\n### Practical tips\nMost people do best with a free digital account as their main spending card (or as a second account alongside a high-street bank for salary and bills). You can open these without switching your existing account. All the major options above are fully authorised UK banks with FSCS protection (up to £120,000 per person per authorised firm).\n\nRates, cashback rules, and eligibility change, so check the latest details directly with the provider or on MoneySavingExpert and Which? before applying. A soft credit check is usually involved for the basic accounts.",
